  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nBenign conditions in place this afternoon, with little to note for \nthe rest of the day today besides fair weather cumulus through the \nafternoon and northwesterly winds gusting up to 20 to 25 miles per \nhour. Clear skies overnight tonight lead to strong radiative cooling \nand near-full humidity recovery by early Thursday morning, \nparticularly over the James River Valley. With winds also expected \nto drop below 5 miles per hour overnight tonight, some pockets for \nfog can't quite be ruled out at this point. However, confidence is \nlow, and any fog that does manage to develop will likely not be \nwidespread. \n\nThe next chances for rain arrive overnight Thursday into Friday, \nwith the best chances (50-70%) coming Friday evening. Severe weather \nmay be possible during that time frame, and a Marginal Risk is in \nplace for the majority of the forecast area. A low pressure center \nwill move into the region and bring a warm front up through the \nforecast area. Perpendicular to this front, a southerly low-level \njet of 40 to 50 knots is also expected to develop, helping to \nprovide forcing for the storms. Anticipating the main severe threat \nto be hail, with a CAPE/Shear combo of 2000 J/kg and 40-50 knots \nexpected. Also expecting DCAPE values to reach over 1000 J/kg, which \nalong with pockets of low-level lapse rates upwards of 8 C/km will \nsupport severe wind as a potential hazard. An early look at tornado \nchances show some disagreement in the potential Friday evening, but \nwith many high-resolution models yet to encompass the event in their \ntime range, this can be taken with a grain of salt for now. The most \nlikely scenario for which a tornado could develop would be a spinup \nalong the warm front. \n\nLooking into next week, there is some signal for the upper-level \nridge to shift eastward at the beginning of the week, allowing for \nan upper-level low to move into the region. This setup could \npotentially bring another round of rain to parts of the area, \nalthough there is still plenty of uncertainty surrounding this \npotential. Latest ensemble guidance peaks around a roughly 25 \npercent chance of rain Monday morning. An early look at model \nsoundings also show a somewhat limited concern for severe weather \npotential at this point. As always with the extended forecast, there \nis plenty of time for things to change on way or the other and for \nconfidence to increase as we move closer to next week.",
